<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=babylonian-astronomy">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Babylonian astronomy</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as astronomy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Babylonian astronomy refers to the systematic observation and mathematical prediction of celestial phenomena developed in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, flourishing under Chaldean patronage. Its records and methods influenced Greek astronomy after Alexander's conquests. The broader astronomical tradition was later continued by scholars in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, at observatories under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a>, and elsewhere.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy.jpg" alt="Babylonian astronomy" width="320" height="516" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Linguica at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Babylonian_tablet_recording_Halley%27s_comet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 1800 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Babylonia</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The article also gives earlier or vaguer dates, none presented as secure: lunar omens in 'reports from Nineveh and Babylon, circa 2500-670 B.C.', Venus observations that 'probably' go back to the second millennium BC but survive in a 7th-century BC copy (the Venus tablet of Ammisaduqa), and the Omen Compendia, composed from the beginning of the second millennium onwards.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">Cuneiform<span>c. 3300 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/sexagesimal">Base-60 numbers<span>3rd millennium BCE</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/heliocentrism">Heliocentrism<span>c. 270 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/antikythera-mechanism">Antikythera mechanism<span>2nd century BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/astrolabe">Astrolabe<span>4th century CE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg-observatory">Ulugh Beg Observatory<span>1420s</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=babylonian-astronomy">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
